\"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvate oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to pyruvate on Fe0-awaruite\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"awaruite (Ni3Fe)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, alkaline, H2-rich serpentinization-driven vent fluids\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated H2 activation and stepwise CO2 reduction with C-C bond formation on Fe-Ni alloy and iron metal surfaces.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) experimentally obtained pyruvate from CO2 and H2 on Fe0/awaruite at 373 K. This node is encoded in conserved form as 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O. It provides a purely geochemical entry into the pyruvate hub; the main limitation is pyruvate thermal lability, so cooler vent mixing zones are more plausible than the hottest effluents (Preiner et al., 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to pyruvate on Fe0-magnetite\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"magnetite (Fe3O4)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, alkaline, H2-rich vent fluids with mixed metal/oxide surfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe0 supplies a highly reducing surface while magnetite adsorbs and activates CO2, enabling sequential reduction and C-C coupling to C3 products.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) reported pyruvate formation from CO2 and H2 with Fe0 plus magnetite at 373 K. The present node uses the atom-balanced net stoichiometry 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O. This is a strong hydrothermal route because both minerals are geochemically plausible in serpentinizing systems, though pyruvate stability still limits the hottest settings (Preiner et al., 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to pyruvate on magnetite alone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite (Fe3O4)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, H2-rich hydrothermal water contacting mixed-valence iron oxide surfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CO2 adsorption and reduction on a mixed-valence iron oxide surface with subsequent carbon-carbon coupling to the C3 alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) also observed pyruvate from CO2 and H2 on magnetite alone. The node is written as the conserved net reaction 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O. Magnetite-only chemistry is especially attractive geologically because the mineral is widespread, although it is generally less reducing than native metal/alloy systems and still faces the pyruvate-stability constraint (Preiner et al., 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+-promoted hydrothermal decarboxylation of oxaloacetate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic mild hydrothermal mixing-zone waters\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+ coordinates the beta-keto dicarboxylate and lowers the barrier for beta-decarboxylation to pyruvate and CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) showed Fe2+-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation in an iron-driven protometabolic network. Writing CO2 explicitly gives the conserved transformation oxaloacetate -> pyruvate + CO2. This route is chemically strong because beta-keto acid decarboxylation is intrinsically favorable at mild hydrothermal temperature (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface cyanosulfidic decarboxylation of oxaloacetate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanosulfidic surface pools with UV-dark cycling and episodic concentration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nonenzymatic beta-decarboxylation of oxaloacetate under cyanosulfidic surface conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson (2021) reported oxaloacetate decarboxylation to pyruvate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions. The present network writes CO2 explicitly for atom conservation. This route usefully links surface photoredox chemistry to the pyruvate hub, although any downstream alanine formation from surface-generated pyruvate requires later concentration or transfer into Fe-rich settings (Ritson, 2021).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Balanced net hydrothermal reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, Fe-rich, H2-bearing hydrothermal microcompartments; local concentration on mineral surfaces mitigates dilution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine-derived imine/oxime formation at the alpha-keto group followed by Fe-assisted reduction to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) experimentally showed Fe0/Fe2+-promoted conversion of pyruvate plus hydroxylamine to alanine at 343 K. To make the node atom-conserving, it is encoded here as the net hydrothermal reduction pyruvate + hydroxylamine + 2 H2 -> alanine + 2 H2O, with Fe0/Fe2+ retained as mineral redox promoters and vent H2 used as the explicit source of reducing equivalents. This bookkeeping correction is chemically reasonable in H2-rich serpentinizing systems but more interpretive than the exact laboratory formulation; hydroxylamine availability remains debated, and no L-selectivity is demonstrated (Muchowska et al., 2019; Preiner et al., 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of pyruvate oxime from pyruvate and hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic hydrothermal microfilms or pores where local concentration favors dehydration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of hydroxylamine to the keto carbonyl followed by dehydration to the oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is speculative but chemically justified. Hydroxylamine normally condenses with alpha-keto acids to give oximes plus water, and such intermediacy is the clearest balanced decomposition of the pyruvate-to-alanine chemistry reported by Muchowska et al. (2019). The provided alanine-specific studies did not directly isolate pyruvate oxime, so this node is included as a mechanistic surrogate rather than a directly demonstrated prebiotic transformation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reduction of pyruvate oxime to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-373K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich hydrothermal water contacting Fe-rich mineral surfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive hydrogenation and deoxygenation of the oxime functionality to the amino acid with water release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is also speculative but chemically reasonable. Reduction of an oxime-like intermediate to an amino acid is a plausible H2/Fe-driven follow-up step in hydrothermal systems, connecting the oxime node back to the experimentally observed Fe-promoted alanine formation of Muchowska et al. (2019) and the H2-activating mineral context of Preiner et al. (2020). It is less directly supported than rxn_006 and likewise does not resolve chirality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface hydrolysis of alanine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet phases of cyanosulfidic evaporitic pools after aminonitrile accumulation; hydrolysis favored during UV-shielded or dark intervals\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration through amide-like intermediates to the carboxylic acid with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support alanine formation by hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile in cyanosulfidic surface chemistry. The balanced encoding is 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3. This is one of the clearest direct prebiotic routes to alanine, although aminonitrile persistence under strong UV and the lack of enantioselectivity remain open issues (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reductive dehydroxylation of serine to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"submarine hydrothermal water with serpentinization-derived H2\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive dehydroxylation of the side-chain hydroxymethyl group to a methyl group with water loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) reported hydrothermal conversion of serine to alanine; the conserved form is serine + H2 -> alanine + H2O. This route is experimentally grounded and geochemically plausible in H2-rich submarine systems, but it depends on prior serine availability and still gives no demonstrated route to L-enrichment (Aubrey et al., 2008).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe0-awaruite vent fixation plus direct net amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-awaruite forms pyruvate, followed by balanced Fe-promoted reductive amination to alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe0-magnetite vent fixation plus direct net amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-magnetite forms pyruvate, then pyruvate undergoes balanced reductive amination to alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Magnetite-only vent fixation plus direct net amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Magnetite alone generates pyruvate from CO2/H2 hydrothermally, followed by direct balanced alanine-forming reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal oxaloacetate decarboxylation plus direct net amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Fe2+-promoted hydrothermal decarboxylation of oxaloacetate gives pyruvate, which then undergoes balanced reductive amination to alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation plus hydrothermal amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface cyanosulfidic decarboxylation of oxaloacetate generates pyruvate, which is then transferred or concentrated into Fe-rich hydrothermal settings for reductive amination to alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe0-awaruite vent fixation plus oxime route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-awaruite gives pyruvate, then a speculative but chemically motivated oxime intermediate is formed and reduced to alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe0-magnetite vent fixation plus oxime route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-magnetite feeds pyruvate into the oxime-mediated alanine-forming sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal oxaloacetate decarboxylation plus oxime route\",\n      \"description\": \"Fe2+-promoted hydrothermal decarboxylation of oxaloacetate supplies pyruvate, which proceeds through the speculative oxime intermediate to alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct surface aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Alanine aminonitrile hydrolyzes directly in cyanosulfidic surface waters to alanine with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal serine reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Serine is reduced under H2-rich hydrothermal conditions to alanine by reductive dehydroxylation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "The network is built around the experimentally well-supported pyruvate hub. Pyruvate is generated by three hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation variants distinguished by mineral assemblage and by two oxaloacetate decarboxylation routes, then converted to alanine either by a balanced net Fe-promoted reductive amination or by a more explicit, partly speculative oxime-mediated sequence. Two orthogonal direct entries bypass pyruvate entirely: surface hydrolysis of alanine aminonitrile and hydrothermal reduction of serine. Ten distinct pathways are enumerated. Because none of the cited routes is intrinsically enantioselective, the network is best read as producing an alanine pool that contains the requested L-alanine, with later L-enrichment unresolved. The weaker lactate-oxidation branch from the review is omitted here because the explicit oxidant/byproduct bookkeeping is underspecified in the curated report, whereas every reaction below is kept atom-balanced.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxosuccinic acid", "common_name": "oxaloacetate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alanine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H7NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "serine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": 