he exact laboratory formulation; hydroxylamine availability remains debated, and no L-selectivity is demonstrated (Muchowska et al., 2019; Preiner et al., 2020)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Formation of pyruvate oxime from pyruvate and hydroxylamine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic hydrothermal microfilms or pores where local concentration favors dehydration"}, "mechanism": "Nucleophilic addition of hydroxylamine to the keto carbonyl followed by dehydration to the oxime.", "reasoning": "This step is speculative but chemically justified. Hydroxylamine normally condenses with alpha-keto acids to give oximes plus water, and such intermediacy is the clearest balanced decomposition of the pyruvate-to-alanine chemistry reported by Muchowska et al. (2019). The provided alanine-specific studies did not directly isolate pyruvate oxime, so this node is included as a mechanistic surrogate rather than a directly demonstrated prebiotic transformation."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrothermal reduction of pyruvate oxime to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["elemental iron (Fe0)", "ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-373K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe0 + Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "H2-rich hydrothermal water contacting Fe-rich mineral surfaces"}, "mechanism": "Reductive hydrogenation and deoxygenation of the oxime functionality to the amino acid with water release.", "reasoning": "This step is also speculative but chemically reasonable. Reduction of an oxime-like intermediate to an amino acid is a plausible H2/Fe-driven follow-up step in hydrothermal systems, connecting the oxime node back to the experimentally observed Fe-promoted alanine formation of Muchowska et al. (2019) and the H2-activating mineral context of Preiner et al. (2020). It is less directly supported than rxn_006 and likewise does not resolve chirality."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Surface hydrolysis of alanine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "wet phases of cyanosulfidic evaporitic pools after aminonitrile accumulation; hydrolysis favored during UV-shielded or dark intervals"}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration through amide-like intermediates to the carboxylic acid with ammonia release.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support alanine formation by hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile in cyanosulfidic surface chemistry. The balanced encoding is 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3. This is one of the clearest direct prebiotic routes to alanine, although aminonitrile persistence under strong UV and the lack of enantioselectivity remain open issues (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrothermal reductive dehydroxylation of serine to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "submarine hydrothermal water with serpentinization-derived H2"}, "mechanism": "Reductive dehydroxylation of the side-chain hydroxymethyl group to a methyl group with water loss.", "reasoning": "Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) reported hydrothermal conversion of serine to alanine; the conserved form is serine + H2 -> alanine + H2O. This route is experimentally grounded and geochemically plausible in H2-rich submarine systems, but it depends on prior serine availability and still gives no demonstrated route to L-enrichment (Aubrey et al., 2008)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Fe0-awaruite vent fixation plus direct net amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-awaruite forms pyruvate, followed by balanced Fe-promoted reductive amination to alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Fe0-magnetite vent fixation plus direct net amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-magnetite forms pyruvate, then pyruvate undergoes balanced reductive amination to alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Magnetite-only vent fixation plus direct net amination", "description": "Magnetite alone generates pyruvate from CO2/H2 hydrothermally, followed by direct balanced alanine-forming reductive amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Hydrothermal oxaloacetate decarboxylation plus direct net amination", "description": "Fe2+-promoted hydrothermal decarboxylation of oxaloacetate gives pyruvate, which then undergoes balanced reductive amination to alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation plus hydrothermal amination", "description": "Surface cyanosulfidic decarboxylation of oxaloacetate generates pyruvate, which is then transferred or concentrated into Fe-rich hydrothermal settings for reductive amination to alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Fe0-awaruite vent fixation plus oxime route", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation on Fe0-awaruite gives pyruvate, then a speculative but chemically motivated oxime intermediate is formed and reduced to alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol