e\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to pyruvate on Fe0-awaruite\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"awaruite (Ni3Fe)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline, H2-rich vent setting; mass-conserving net stoichiometry is 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe/Ni metal surfaces adsorb CO2 and activate H2, enabling stepwise reduction and C-C coupling to a C3 alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) experimentally obtained pyruvate from CO2 and H2 at 100C on Fe0 and awaruite. This is the strongest purely geochemical upstream route to the pyruvate hub under serpentinization-like conditions. The node is written in balanced net form; pyruvate thermal lability argues for the mild end of hydrothermal conditions rather than hotter black-smoker regimes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to pyruvate on Fe0-magnetite\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"magnetite (Fe3O4)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, H2-rich vent setting; mass-conserving net stoichiometry is 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Native iron activates H2 while magnetite provides an adsorbing oxide surface for stepwise CO2 reduction and carbon-carbon bond formation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) also reported pyruvate formation from CO2 and H2 using Fe0 plus magnetite at 100C. Magnetite is common in hydrothermal systems and changes the catalytic surface ensemble enough to justify a separate pathway variant while remaining experimentally grounded.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to pyruvate on magnetite alone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite (Fe3O4)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic hydrothermal water; mass-conserving net stoichiometry is 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mixed-valence iron oxide surfaces adsorb CO2 and support sequential reduction and coupling steps to a C3 alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) further showed that magnetite alone can support pyruvate formation from CO2 and H2. This route is retained as a distinct catalyst-specific pathway because catalyst identity affects product selectivity and geochemical accessibility.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+-catalyzed oxaloacetate decarboxylation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mild submarine hydrothermal mixing zone\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+ coordinates the beta-keto dicarboxylate and lowers the barrier to beta-decarboxylation, releasing CO2 and forming pyruvate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) reported Fe2+-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation at 70C in water. This is a thermodynamically favorable route into the pyruvate hub and strengthens the metabolic-style connectivity of the alanine network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic oxaloacetate decarboxylation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanosulfidic surface pool, UV-exposed, wet-dry cycling; reported on roughly hour timescales\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condition-assisted beta-keto-acid decarboxylation within the cyanosulfidic Krebs-cycle analogue releases CO2 and forms pyruvate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson (2021) showed oxaloacetate decarboxylation to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic surface conditions. This provides a surface source of the same pyruvate hub; any continuation into iron-driven amination requires later concentration or transfer into an Fe-rich niche.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-promoted reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 + Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich hydrothermal mixing zone; balanced net stoichiometry is pyruvate + NH2OH + 2 H2 -> alanine + 2 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto group and iron-mediated reduction removes the N-O bond to furnish the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) demonstrated iron-promoted conversion of pyruvate and hydroxylamine to alanine at 70C. Because the experimental transformation is commonly written without explicit redox bookkeeping, this network encodes the balanced net reaction with H2 as the hydrothermal reducing equivalent and Fe0/Fe2+ as the experimentally supported mediators. The review notes that hydroxylamine availability remains debated, and no intrinsic enantioselection was shown, so the product is treated as rac-alanine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate oxime formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"hydrothermal pores or low-water-activity microenvironments; balanced net stoichiometry is pyruvate + NH2OH -> pyruvate oxime + H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of hydroxylamine with the alpha-keto carbonyl yields an oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Muchowska et al. (2019) support the overall pyruvate plus hydroxylamine to alanine chemistry, and oxime formation from alpha-keto acids and hydroxylamine is standard carbonyl chemistry, but a discrete pyruvate oxime intermediate was not isolated in the provided alanine studies. It is included only as a chemically plausible, mass-conserving alternative branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of pyruvate oxime to rac-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"awaruite (Ni3Fe)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 or Ni3Fe surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich hydrothermal pores; balanced net stoichiometry is pyruvate oxime + 2 H2 -> alanine + H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-mediated H2 activation drives oxime hydrogenation and N-O bond cleavage to the amine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Preiner et al. (2020) show that Fe/Ni mineral surfaces activate H2 in hydrothermal settings, and Muchowska et al. (2019) show that iron-rich hydrothermal systems can reduce the pyruvate/hydroxylamine system to alanine. Combining those results makes oxime hydrogenation plausible, but this specific step has not been directly demonstrated for alanine in the provided literature and should be treated as lower-confidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reduction of serine to rac-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-470K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"submarine hydrothermal, H2-rich, anoxic; balanced net stoichiometry is serine + H2 -> alanine + H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive dehydroxylation converts the side-chain hydroxymethyl group of serine into the methyl group of alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aubrey et al. (2008) reported serine reduction to alanine under submarine hydrothermal conditions with H2. Because prebiotic serine pools would not be enantiopure, the network conservatively treats the immediate product as rac-alanine even though laboratory shorthand often refers to L-serine to L-alanine conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Strecker synthesis of alanine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich cyanosulfidic pool with wet-dry cycling and intermittent UV; balanced net stoichiometry is acetaldehyde + HCN + NH3 -> alanine aminonitrile + H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde forms an imine or iminium with ammonia, then cyanide addition furnishes the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) place alanine aminonitrile within a cyanosulfidic Strecker-like framework that unifies amino acid, nucleotide, and lipid precursor chemistry. The explicit acetaldehyde plus HCN plus NH3 bookkeeping used here is standard chemistry consistent with that framework, although the review emphasizes the downstream hydrolysis evidence more directly. Aminonitrile persistence under UV and variable pH remains a recognized limitation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of alanine aminonitrile to rac-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"wet phase of a cyanosulfidic surface pool; balanced net stoichiometry is alanine aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration gives an amide intermediate, then further hydrolysis yields the carboxylic acid with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) experimentally support hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile to alanine under aqueous prebiotic conditions. The route is retained as a direct surface pathway into the alanine pool, but as noted in the review it does not solve chirality and the environmental stability of the aminonitrile remains an open question.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-interface phase resolution of rac-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"mineral interfaces (speculative chiral adsorption/crystallization)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"mineral interface\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry or geothermal cycling; formal balanced representation is 2 DL-alanine -> L-alanine + D-alanine\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Physical phase partitioning enriches one enantiomer relative to the other through preferential adsorption or crystallization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and included only because the requested target is L-alanine. The provided synthesis studies support access to alanine pools but not intrinsic homochirality (Patel et al., 2015; Muchowska et al., 2019; Aubrey et al., 2008; Preiner et al., 2020; Ritson, 2021). This node therefore represents an unresolved post-synthetic enantioenrichment step rather than experimentally secured alanine synthesis chemistry.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-awaruite pyruvate hub route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 and H2 fixation on Fe0-awaruite gives pyruvate, Fe-mediated reductive amination gives rac-alanine, and a speculative mineral-interface resolution yields L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-magnetite pyruvate hub route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 and H2 fixation on Fe0-magnetite gives pyruvate, followed by iron-promoted reductive amination and later phase resolution to the L-enantiomer.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"p